𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗼 𝘆𝗼𝘂 𝘃𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗥𝗘𝗦𝗧 𝗔𝗣𝗜 𝗿𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗽𝗮𝘆𝗹𝗼𝗮𝗱𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗦𝗽𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗕𝗼𝗼𝘁? 🚀

Accepting a JSON request is easy.

Making sure the incoming data is valid is what matters.

In Spring Boot, we can use Jakarta Bean Validation to validate request payloads without writing repetitive validation logic in every controller.

Here's how 👇

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗔𝗱𝗱 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗱𝗲𝗽𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗰𝘆

Add "spring-boot-starter-validation" to your project.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗔𝗱𝗱 𝘃𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗮𝗻𝗻𝗼𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀

public record CreateUserRequest(
    @NotBlank(message = "Name is required")
    String name,

    @NotBlank(message = "Email is required")
    @Email(message = "Invalid email format")
    String email,

    @Min(value = 18, message = "Must be at least 18")
    Integer age
) {}

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗨𝘀𝗲 "@Valid" 𝗶𝗻 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗰𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗿

@PostMapping("/users")
public ResponseEntity<String> createUser(
        @Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest request) {
    return ResponseEntity.ok("User created");
}

If the payload violates a constraint, Spring MVC raises a validation exception instead of proceeding normally with the controller method.

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗥𝗲𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗻 𝗮 𝗰𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿 𝟰𝟬𝟬 𝗲𝗿𝗿𝗼𝗿

Use "@RestControllerAdvice" to handle validation exceptions and return a consistent error response that tells the client which fields need correction.

𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗱𝗼𝗲𝘀 𝘁𝗵𝗶𝘀 𝗺𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿?

✅ Cleaner controller code
✅ Consistent API error responses
✅ Fewer invalid requests reaching business logic
✅ Better API usability and maintainability

𝗣𝗿𝗼 𝘁𝗶𝗽: "@NotBlank", "@NotNull", "@NotEmpty" and "@Email" are not interchangeable. Choose the constraint that matches your requirement.

Request validation is only one layer. Business rules, authorization and database constraints still need appropriate checks.

💬 Question for Java developers: Do you handle validation errors using "@RestControllerAdvice", or do you use another approach?

